Name": "Woody Guthrie", "verb": "DEFINES", "targetSlug": "folk-music", "targetName": "Folk Music", "context": "Woody Guthrie's contributions in the early 20th century helped define the narrative and thematic elements of American folk music."}], "places": [{"name": "Newport, USA", "role": "Major Festival"}, {"name": "New York City, USA", "role": "Cultural Hub"}, {"name": "London, UK", "role": "Musical Influence"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: folk music", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "folk-music"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q43343", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Folk_music"], "tags": ["music genre", "Music & Compositions", "cause-and-effect"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "folk music left a notable mark on Classical history, documented across 71 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Folk music has significantly shaped cultural identities and social movements, influencing various genres and inspiring countless artists worldwide.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-06-09T13:33:11Z", "editorId": "gpt-4o-mini·cloud·GH#27209607796", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Music meta-genre encompassing traditional folk music, contemporary folk music (evolved from the former during the 20th-century folk revival), and derivatives of these two branches", "newValue": "Folk music is a rich and diverse musical genre that encompasses traditional and contemporary forms, emerging from the shared cultural heritage of communities around the world. Dating back to ancient times, folk music served as a medium for storytelling, cultural expression, and social cohesion, with its roots traceable to the oral traditions of various civilizations. It gained prominence in the 20th century during the folk revival, which saw influential figures such as Woody Guthrie and Pete Seeger in the United States bring traditional songs to the forefront of popular culture, highlighting social issues and the human experience.

As industrialization and urbanization transformed societies, folk music adapted, preserving the stories of the past while also evolving to reflect contemporary themes. The Folk Revival of the 1930s to the 1960s in Western countries was characterized by a resurgence of interest in traditional music, leading to festivals, collectives, and the birth of new genres like folk rock. Prominent events such as the Newport Folk Festival in 1959 showcased artists who interpreted and redefined folk music for new audiences, exemplifying its role as a vehicle for social change and political expression.

